    reconcile(maintain): close the SKILL_CHECKS-absence gap, drop the docstring's own restated copy
    
    wave-14 monitor findings on ed3c/skill-concerns#62:
    
    - build_pass() used run_all.SKILL_CHECKS.get(skill, ()) to gather proof, so a
      skill entirely absent from SKILL_CHECKS produced the identical report shape
      (proof=[], zero proof_findings) as a skill whose declared row ran green.
      Absence now blocks with a distinct diagnostic (BUILD_SKILL_UNCHECKED)
      instead of reading as vacuously proven. New planted negative in --selftest
      covers it end to end (19/19 PASS); the two pre-existing demo-subject
      positive-control passes now register a real check.py from their first
      commit instead of accidentally relying on the same absence-as-proof gap.
    
    - The module docstring restated AGENTS.md's three sc#59 adjudications in
      full prose with no mechanical reader of its own - exactly the "restated
      copy is the drift the split exists to prevent" anti-pattern AGENTS.md
      itself names. Replaced with a pointer; AGENTS.md stays the source and
      tests/test_maintain_skills.py::test_maintain_docs_carry_the_sc59_adjudications
      is unchanged (it already only reads AGENTS.md).
    
    - Tightened the "writes may land only under skills/<skill>/" edit-scope
      claim (module docstring + AGENTS.md) to name its actual boundary: a
      git-status diff scoped to this checkout, blind to writes at an absolute
      path outside it, and blind to a concurrent actor holding the same
      checkout. Filed as ed3c/skill-concerns#66 (not fixed here - a real fix is
      a larger redesign than this reconcile pass should carry into freshly
      landed, selftest-passing code).
    
    python3 scripts/maintain_skills.py --selftest -> 19/19 PASS
    python3 scripts/run_all.py -> skill-concerns: PASS
    
    Refs ed3c/skill-concerns#62

